@page "/blogs/using-docker-test-containers-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi"
@inherits BasePage
@using BaseComponents

<Content FileName=@nameof(UsingDockerTestContainersInFunctionalTestingInASPNET)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
        <p>
            In this article, let's learn about testing with <Highlight>real database</Highlight> using 
            <Highlight>Docker Test Containers</Highlight> in <Highlight>Web API</Highlight> in ASP.NET Core on <Highlight>.NET 11</Highlight>.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong>Note:</strong> If you have not done so already, I recommend you read the article on
            <NavLink href="blogs/functional-testing-your-asp-net-webapi" Match="NavLinkMatch.All">Functional testing your ASP.NET WEB API</NavLink>.
        </p>

        <p>
            We are all aware that the softwares we build works rarely in isolation. Our apps will talk to many external systems like 
            <Highlight>database, message brokers, web browsers, cloud services or just any other external systems</Highlight>. And validating the 
            integration with these external systems is very important. 
        </p>

        <p>
            <Highlight>Testcontainers</Highlight> is a library that provides easy and lightweight APIs for bootstrapping local development and 
            test dependencies with <Highlight>real services</Highlight> wrapped in <Highlight>Docker containers</Highlight>. Using 
            Testcontainers, you can write tests that depend on the <Highlight>same services you use in production without mocks or in-memory services.</Highlight>
        </p>

        

        </What>

        <Why>

        <p>
            <Highlight>Cloud-native infrastructure</Highlight> and <Highlight>microservices</Highlight> have taken control away from 
            developers and made it painful to work locally. While you own only your Service and its datastore, you have several downstream dependencies that you 
            need for local development and integration testing. This brings the following challenges:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                Before running tests, you must <Highlight>ensure that the infrastructure is up and running</Highlight> and is 
                <Highlight>pre-configured in a desired state</Highlight>.
            </li>
            <li>
                If the <Highlight>resources (database, message broker, etc) are shared across multiple users or CI pipelines</Highlight>, then the 
                <Highlight>test results are non-deterministic</Highlight> because of the possibility of data corruption and configuration drift.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            A <Highlight>possible workaround could be using in-memory variations of the required services</Highlight>. But this is almost similar 
            like using mocks. In-memory services might not have all the features of the production / real service amd they have their own problems.
        </p>

        <p>
            <Highlight>Testcontainers solves this by using Docker to spin up real services</Highlight> for functional testing and helps executing 
            <Highlight>reliable and repeatable tests</Highlight> by talking to those real services and providing a programmatic API for your test 
            code.
        </p>

        

        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4 id="steps-to-do-functional-test-using-test-containers">Steps to do Functional Test using Test Containers</h4>

        <p>
            Let's learn how to do functional test using Test Containers in ASP.NET Web API. We will be using the same project we used in the previous article 
            and <Highlight>try using test containers for database instead of in-memory database</Highlight>.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                Install <Highlight>Testcontainers.PostgreSql</Highlight> and <Highlight>Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL</Highlight>
                in your functional test project. Match the PostgreSQL provider's supported EF Core major version to the API's EF Core packages.
                These examples target <Highlight>.NET 11</Highlight> and use <Highlight>EF Core 11.0.0</Highlight>,
                rather than the linked sample's older configuration.
                Complete the <NavLink href="blogs/faking-dependencies-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi#functional-test-prerequisites">coordinated package and xUnit v3 migration</NavLink>
                first, including executable test-project output and a compatible Visual Studio adapter.
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Replace the SQLite <Highlight>AppFixture</Highlight> from the
                    <NavLink href="blogs/faking-dependencies-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi">faking dependencies article</NavLink>
                    with the assembly fixture below. Keep that article's <Highlight>BaseTest</Highlight>, which owns a client and
                    database scope per test. The fixture now owns both the application host and the PostgreSQL container.
                </p>

                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using API.DbContexts;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Infrastructure;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Extensions;
using Testcontainers.PostgreSql;
using Xunit;

[assembly: AssemblyFixture(typeof(FunctionalTest.AppFixture))]
namespace FunctionalTest;
public class AppFixture : WebApplicationFactory&lt;Program&gt;, IAsyncLifetime
{
    public string PostgresConnectionString =&gt; _dbContainer.GetConnectionString();
    private readonly PostgreSqlContainer _dbContainer =
        new PostgreSqlBuilder("postgres:17-alpine")
        .WithDatabase("ILoveDotNet")
        .WithUsername("postgres")
        .WithPassword("postgres")
        .Build();

    protected override void ConfigureWebHost(IWebHostBuilder builder)
    {
        builder.UseEnvironment("Test");
        builder.ConfigureTestServices(services =&gt;
        {
            services.RemoveAll&lt;WeatherForecastDbContext&gt;();
            services.RemoveAll&lt;DbContextOptions&lt;WeatherForecastDbContext&gt;&gt;();
            services.RemoveAll&lt;IDbContextOptionsConfiguration&lt;WeatherForecastDbContext&gt;&gt;();
            services.AddDbContext&lt;WeatherForecastDbContext&gt;(opts =&gt;
                opts.UseNpgsql(PostgresConnectionString));
        });
    }

    public async ValueTask InitializeAsync()
    {
        await _dbContainer.StartAsync();
        await using var scope = Services.CreateAsyncScope();
        var db = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService&lt;WeatherForecastDbContext&gt;();
        await db.Database.MigrateAsync();
        await DatabaseHelper.InitializeDbForTestsAsync(db);
    }

    public override async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await base.DisposeAsync();
        await _dbContainer.DisposeAsync();
    }
}
                </CodeSnippet>

                

                <p>
                    In the above code, we are <Highlight>initializing</Highlight> the database container and getting the 
                    <Highlight>connection string</Highlight> from the container and exposing it to be used in 
                    <Highlight>ConfigureTestServices</Highlight> in <Highlight>AppFixture</Highlight>. Only access
                    <Highlight>Services</Highlight> after the container starts: this builds the shared application host.
                    We then apply migrations and initialize seed data using a short-lived scope.
                    Use PostgreSQL-compatible migrations from the API project, not migrations generated for a different provider.
                    The application host is disposed before the container after all tests finish.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Finally we just need to run the tests and see the magic happening. We can see the <Highlight>database container spinning up</Highlight> 
                    and running and our tests are running against the real database within test container.
                </p>
                
                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using API;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;
using System.Net;
using Xunit;

namespace FunctionalTest;
[ExcludeFromCodeCoverage]
public class WeatherForecastControllerShouldTests(
    AppFixture fixture,
    ITestOutputHelper outputHelper) : BaseTest(fixture)
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task ReturnExpectedResponse_From_Database()
    {
        var expectedWeatherForecast = await Database.WeatherForecasts.FirstAsync();

        var result = await Client.GetJsonResultAsync&lt;List&lt;WeatherForecast&gt;&gt;("/weatherforecast/fromdatabase", HttpStatusCode.OK, outputHelper);
        Assert.Equivalent(expectedWeatherForecast, Assert.Single(result));
    }
}
                </CodeSnippet>

                
            </li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            xUnit injects the registered assembly fixture directly, without <Highlight>[Collection]</Highlight> or
            <Highlight>IClassFixture</Highlight>. Assembly fixtures do not disable parallel execution.
            Keep shared seed data read-only, and isolate any test that writes data. The lifetime is now:
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>Create the assembly's AppFixture.</li>
            <li>Start the PostgreSQL container once.</li>
            <li>Run Program.cs.</li>
            <li>Service Registration.</li>
            <li>Overriding Configuration.</li>
            <li>Build Web Application.</li>
            <li>Apply migrations and seed data once.</li>
            <li>Create each test with its own client and database scope.</li>
            <li>Test Code Execution.</li>
            <li>Dispose the shared application host, then the container, after all tests finish.</li>
        </ol>

